t listed do not exist in this environment and cannot be installed or obtained. Review what is available and use whatever helps you complete and check your work. If execution tools are provided, they give you a live development environment: the commands you run through them execute for real in your workspace, on a machine with Node.js and npm installed and the npm registry reachable, so the packages you install and the servers you start are real. If no installation or execution tools are provided, author every required file completely from first principles; dependencies declared in package.json files are provisioned by the environment after you finish.<|tool>declaration:read_file{description:<|\"|>Read a file in the workspace.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:write_file{description:<|\"|>Create or overwrite a file. Any missing parent directories in `path` are created automatically.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{content:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>,<|\"|>content<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:edit_file{description:<|\"|>Replace a unique substring in a file.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{new_str:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},old_str:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>,<|\"|>old_str<|\"|>,<|\"|>new_str<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:list_dir{description:<|\"|>List a directory in the workspace.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:finish{description:<|\"|>Signal that the task is complete.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{summary:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:run_command{description:<|\"|>Run a shell command in the workspace and see its stdout/stderr.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{command:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>command<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:run_background_command{description:<|\"|>Run a shell command in the workspace in the background and return its PID. Useful for long-running processes like dev servers.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{command:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>command<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:run_command_and_capture_output{description:<|\"|>Run a command for a maximum duration and return its captured output. Useful for commands where you just want to sample output for a few seconds. NOT for starting servers - use run_background_command for those.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{command:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},duration:{type:<|\"|>INTEGER<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>command<|\"|>,<|\"|>duration<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:list_background_commands{description:<|\"|>List background processes you started in this run, with their PIDs and the ports they are listening on.<|\"|>,parameters:{type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:stop_background_command{description:<|\"|>Stop a background process you started earlier (e.g. to free its port before restarting a server).<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{pid:{type:<|\"|>INTEGER<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>pid<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><turn|>\n<|turn>user\n## Context\n\nWe are building a traditional client-server web application with real-time capabilities. To minimize external dependencies while retaining the power of PostgreSQL, we are using PGLite embedded within a Node.js server. Real-time updates will be pushed to the client using Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- Provide a simple message board where users can post text messages.\n- Ensure all connected clients receive updates instantly when new messages are posted.\n- Store data persistently on the local disk using PGLite.\n- Keep the frontend incredibly simple and lightweight.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- User authentication and authorization.\n- Rich text formatting or media attachments.\n- Horizontal scaling across multiple server instances (PGLite is tied to the single Node instance).\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Decision 1: Embedded PGLite vs External Postgres**\n- *Rationale*: PGLite allows us to run PostgreSQL directly inside the Node.js process. This means users don't need to install or configure a separate database service. It seamlessly handles raw SQL queries and can persist to the filesystem.\n\n**Decision 2: Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets**\n- *Rationale*: We only need one-way real-time communication (Server pushing new messages to Clients). Clients can post new messages via standard HTTP POST requests. SSE is much simpler to implement, works natively over standard HTTP, and doesn't require a complex WebSocket library like Socket.io.\n\n**Decision 3: Vanilla JS / Vite vs React**\n- *Rationale*: Given the minimal UI requirements (a message list and a text input), Vanilla JS keeps the build extremely fast and the dependency tree small. Vite provides a great developer experience out of the box.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Risk] PGLite performance on very large datasets: Embedded WASM databases are incredibly fast but might struggle if the dataset grows to tens of gigabytes.\n  - *Mitigation*: This is a local application and we expect message volume to be manageable for a single server instance.\n- [Risk] SSE connection limits: Browsers have limits on the number of concurrent SSE connections per domain.\n  - *Mitigation*: We only need one SSE connection per client tab, which is well within browser limits.\n\n## Why\n\nWe need a fast, local-first collaborative message board that allows users to post messages and see updates in real-time. This provides an incredibly low-latency experience by keeping data local while ensuring all users see the same state instantly. It serves as an excellent demonstration of using embedded PGLite combined with Server-Sent Events (SSE) to create a robust, lightweight realtime application without external database dependencies.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- We are introducing a brand new traditional client-server web application.\n- The backend will be a Node.js server using an embedded PGLite database for raw SQL queries, persisting data to the local disk.\n- Real-time synchronization will be handled via Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n- The frontend will be a simple, lightweight Single Page Application (Vanilla JS or React via Vite).\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `realtime-board`: The core message board capability covering posting messages, viewing a message feed, and receiving live updates via SSE.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\nNone (Greenfield project).\n\n## Impact\n\nThis is a brand new project. It will establish the foundational architecture including the Node.js backend setup, the embedded PGLite database structure, the SSE connection handling, and the frontend web UI.\n\n## 1. Project Initialization\n\n- [ ] 1.1 Initialize the Node.js project and install backend dependencies (express, @electric-sql/pglite, cors)\n- [ ] 1.2 Initialize the Vanilla JS / Vite project for the frontend and install frontend dependencies\n- [ ] 1.3 Set up basic npm scripts for concurrently running backend and frontend development servers\n- [ ] 1.4 Make the app start from a fresh checkout with no manual steps: once the dependencies declared in its package.json files are installed, `npm run dev` at the project root starts both the backend and the frontend, the backend creates any directory it needs (such as PGLite's data directory), and the page the frontend serves reaches the backend's API and event stream\n\n## 2. Backend Infrastructure & Database\n\n- [ ] 2.1 Set up a basic Express server with CORS and JSON body parsing\n- [ ] 2.2 Initialize embedded PGLite configured to write to the local file system\n- [ ] 2.3 Create the database initialization script (create `messages` table with `id`, `text`, `created_at`)\n- [ ] 2.4 Create the historical messages endpoint (`GET /api/messages`) to fetch initial state\n\n## 3. Real-time Capabilities (SSE)\n\n- [ ] 3.1 Implement the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) that maintains active connections with clients\n- [ ] 3.2 Implement the messaging endpoint (`POST /api/messages`) to insert text into PGLite\n- [ ] 3.3 Broadcast new messages to all active SSE connections when a new message is successfully inserted\n\n## 4. Frontend Implementation\n\n- [ ] 4.1 Create the basic HTML layout containing a message list and a text input form\n- [ ] 4.2 Fetch the message history (`GET /api/messages`) on initial page load and render messages\n- [ ] 4.3 Connect to the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) using `EventSource` to listen for new messages\n- [ ] 4.4 Append incoming real-time messages to the DOM seamlessly\n- [ ] 4.5 Wire up the form submission to send a `POST` request to `/api/messages` and clear the input<turn|>\n<|turn>model\n<|tool_call>call:run_command{command:<|\"|>mkdir -p backend frontend<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:run_command{value:<|\"|>$ mkdir -p backend frontend\n[exit 0]\n<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"message-board-backend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"main\": \"index.js\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@electric-sql/pglite\": \"^0.2.0\",\n    \"cors\": \"^2.8.5\",\n    \"express\": \"^4.18.2\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"node index.js\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>backend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 241 chars to backend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"message-board\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"concurrently \\\"npm run dev:backend\\\" \\\"npm run dev:frontend\\\"\",\n    \"dev:backend\": \"cd backend && npm install && npm start\",\n    \"dev:frontend\": \"cd frontend && npm install && npm run dev\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"concurrently\": \"^8.2.2\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 349 chars to package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:run_command{command:<|\"|>cd frontend && npm init -y<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:run_command{value:<|\"|>$ cd frontend && npm init -y\n[exit 0]\nSTDOUT:\nWrote to /tmp/runs/smokes/gemma-q4/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/frontend/package.json:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"frontend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"main\": \"index.js\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"echo \\\"Error: no test specified\\\" && exit 1\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [],\n  \"type\": \"commonjs\"\n}\n\n\n\n<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:edit_file{new_str:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"message-board-frontend\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"vite build\",\n    \"preview\": \"vite preview\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {},\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"vite\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,old_str:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"frontend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"main\": \"index.js\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"echo \\\"Error: no test specified\\\" && exit 1\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [],\n  \"type\": \"commonjs\"\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>frontend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:edit_file{value:<|\"|>edited frontend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>const express = require('express');\nconst cors = require('cors');\nconst { PGLite } = require('@electric-sql/pglite');\nconst path = require('path');\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3001;\n\napp.use(cors());\napp.use(express.json());\n\n// Initialize PGLite with a local directory for persistence\nconst db = new PGLite('./pgdata');\n\n// Database initialization\nasync function initDb() {\n  await db.exec(`\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (\n      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n      text TEXT NOT NULL,\n      created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n    );\n  `);\n  console.log('Database initialized');\n}\n\ninitDb().catch(err => console.error('Error initializing database:', err));\n\n// SSE clients management\nlet clients = [];\n\nfunction broadcast(data) {\n  const message = `data: ${JSON.stringify(data)}\\n\\n`;\n  clients.forEach(client => client.res.write(message));\n}\n\n// Endpoint to get historical messages\napp.get('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query('SELECT * FROM messages ORDER BY created_at ASC');\n    res.json(result.rows);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('Error fetching messages:', err);\n    res.status(500).send('Internal Server Error');\n  }\n});\n\n// SSE endpoint\napp.get('/api/stream', (req, res) => {\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');\n  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');\n  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');\n  res.flushHeaders();\n\n  const client = { res };\n  clients.push(client);\n\n  req.on('close', () => {\n    clients = clients.filter(c => c!== client);\n  });\n});\n\n// Endpoint to post a new message\napp.post('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  const { text } = req.body;\n  if (!text) {\n    return res.status(400).send('Text is required');\n  }\n\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(\n      'INSERT INTO messages (text) VALUES ($1) RETURNING *',\n      [text]\n    );\n    const newMessage = result.rows[0];\n    \n    // Broadcast the new message to all connected SSE clients\n    broadcast(newMessage);\n\n    res.status(201).json(newMessage);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error('Error inserting message:', err);\n    res.status(500).send('Internal Server Error');\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () => {\n  console.log(`Backend server listening at http://localhost:${port}`);\n});\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>backend/index.js<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 2290 chars to backend/index.js<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\n","generated":""}
